// Sri Lanka's Law Aptitude Test and Kenya's Law Aptitude Test share an English
// name and almost nothing else. The Sri Lankan paper is run by Sri Lanka Law
// College for its own admissions, and its answers described A/L passes, a
// Sri Lankan citizenship frame and reserved-seat category certificates. None of
// that applies to a Kenyan applicant, who is sitting the Kenya School of Law's
// test. Written from the Kenya School of Law's published admissions
// requirements and this site's own Kenya LAT data file.
EXAM_FAQS['law-apt-kenya'] = [
  { question: "Who runs the Law Aptitude Test in Kenya, and is it the same exam as Sri Lanka's?", answer: "No, they are two different tests that happen to share an English name. The Kenyan one is run by the Kenya School of Law, the body that trains advocates for the Kenyan legal profession, and its admissions portal is ksl.ac.ke. Sri Lanka's test of the same name belongs to Sri Lanka Law College and serves that country's own admissions. Search results mix the two constantly because both carry the same English title and both lean on language ability, so establish which regulator issued a document before you plan around it. Naming the issuer is the fastest way to sort them: a notice from Sri Lanka Law College contains nothing that applies to your application, whatever else it says. Source: ksl.ac.ke" },
  { question: "What does the Kenya Law Aptitude Test actually test?", answer: "The test draws on two subject groups. The first pairs English ability with General Knowledge, covering comprehension, grammar and vocabulary alongside current affairs, history and politics. The second pairs Logical Reasoning with Legal Awareness, covering analytical thinking and argument analysis alongside constitutional law basics, human rights and the institutions of the Kenyan legal system. The practical consequence is that part of the paper rewards reasoning speed while the rest rewards specific recall, so a candidate who revises only the legal half leaves a large group under-prepared. Paper count, item formats and sitting time have all been described differently by pages that were not quoting the school, so take the current structure from ksl.ac.ke rather than from a revision guide." },
  { question: "What qualifications does the Kenya School of Law expect before you can sit?", answer: "The expected background is a KCSE holder who also holds a bachelor's degree, which is the usual route into the school's Diploma in Law programme. International and equivalent qualifications are also accepted on the school's own terms, which is why A-Level candidates appear in Kenyan legal admissions; note that this is A-Level, and it is a different credential from the Sri Lankan A/L, so do not treat the two labels as the same thing. The Kenya School of Law sets and publishes the grade thresholds it expects for each intake, and those thresholds have been described inconsistently by third-party pages that copy each other rather than the school. Read the current admissions requirements on ksl.ac.ke before you register, and check your result against the bar stated there for your own intake. Source: ksl.ac.ke" },
  { question: "How do you apply, and which documents should you hold on to?", answer: "Applications run through the Kenya School of Law's own admissions portal rather than through a national examinations body, and the school publishes the application window, the intake and the sitting date on ksl.ac.ke. Those dates are set per admission cycle and they move, so treat any date you read on a coaching or news page as unverified until you have seen it on the school's own site. Because such forms ask you to attest to your own results, hold on to your KCSE, A-Level or diploma documentation until the admission list is published rather than filing it and assuming it is safe to discard. Keep the payment receipt alongside it. If a deadline you have seen does not appear on ksl.ac.ke, assume it is either out of date or belongs to a different regulator's cycle entirely." }
];
// WASSCE (Ghana) is NOT a country-variant of Nigeria's WAEC. Ghana runs its own
// entry windows, result dates and certificate process, and University of Ghana
// admission counts six subjects on an aggregate. Aliasing this key to 'waec'
// served naira-PIN registration, a five-credit rule and Nigeria's three diets to
// Ghanaian candidates on a page measured at 1,735 impressions for the query
// "how many subjects are written in wassce". Written from waecgh.org's WASSCE
// school and private pages and UG's published entry requirements.
EXAM_FAQS['wassce'] = [
  { question: "How many subjects do you actually write in the WASSCE in Ghana?", answer: "A school candidate sits a minimum of seven and at most eight papers. WAEC Ghana fixes four core subjects for every candidate \u2014 English Language, Integrated Science, Mathematics (Core) and Social Studies \u2014 and each candidate then adds three or four elective subjects drawn from a single programme option, so the total is 7 or 8 depending on how many electives you pick. The programmes are Agricultural, Business, Technical, Vocational and General, and General splits into General Arts and General Science options. Because the elective count is yours to set, taking four rather than three compresses the revision weeks left before the May/June sitting. Settle the programme option with your school before entry closes, because it is not a decision you can defer to the exam hall. Source: waecgh.org" },
  { question: "When is WASSCE written in Ghana, and who is responsible for registering the candidate?", answer: "WAEC Ghana runs WASSCE twice a year: school candidates write in May/June and private candidates in September/October. At school level registration is not the candidate's job. The entry period runs September to November and lasts six weeks, during which school authorities register their candidates and upload entry data online; Continuous Assessment scores reach the Council on CDs, and completed entry documents with proof of registration-fee payment are submitted to the Council's office for the region. Private candidates register for themselves, online only, between February and May. If you are working from a national portal PIN deadline, you are following Nigeria's registration rules rather than Ghana's. Source: waecgh.org" },
  { question: "Which WASSCE grades count as a credit, and what aggregate does the University of Ghana ask for?", answer: "Results are reported on WAEC's nine-point scale running from A1 at the top to F9 at the bottom, and the University of Ghana counts a credit as A1 to C6 in WASSCE, or A to D in SSSCE. UG asks for at least credits in the four core subjects \u2014 English, Core Mathematics, Integrated Science or Core Science, and Social Studies \u2014 plus three elective subjects, with the total aggregate across those six subjects not exceeding 24 for regular admission. Science-related programmes additionally ask for C4 or better in Elective Mathematics alongside two other science electives. Work out your own aggregate as soon as mock results arrive: UG adds six subjects, not the eight you sit. Source: admissions.ug.edu.gh" },
  { question: "How do you choose WASSCE electives in Ghana, and which subjects carry a practical?", answer: "Electives are not a free choice. WAEC requires you to pick ONE programme \u2014 Agricultural, Business, Technical, Vocational or General \u2014 and then enter for three or four electives from a single option inside that programme, not one from each option. General Arts offers Christian Religious Studies, Literature-in-English, French, Arabic, Geography, Government, History, Economics and a Ghanaian language; General Science requires Elective Mathematics plus two or three of Biology, Chemistry, Physics, French or Music, Geography, General Agriculture and ICT. Business splits into Accounting and Secretarial, and Vocational into Home Economics and Visual Arts. Science subjects carry a laboratory practical alongside the written paper, so lab reporting and graph drawing are examinable, not decoration. Source: waecgh.org" },
  { question: "What happens after WASSCE results are released \u2014 checking results, script review and certificates?", answer: "School-candidate results are released in July so successful candidates can enter tertiary institutions in the same academic year, and private-candidate results are released in December; both are checked online at waecgh.org with your access card. Scripts are kept for three months after results are released and are then destroyed, and a review of scripts is allowed for a fee inside that three-month window only, so decide whether you want one before the clock runs out. Private-candidate certificates are printed on request through waecgh.org and are produced once, meaning a certificate lost or damaged after collection is not replaced. Candidates who lose one can instead request confirmation of results or attestation from the Council. Source: waecgh.org" }
];
