// NCLEX (US/Canada nursing licensure). Added 2205Z to replace the generic
// fallback, which asserted no conducting body and no registration mechanics.
// Every claim below is taken from ncsbn.org or the Pearson VUE candidate
// bulletin. Deliberately NOT stated: exam dates, fees, and item counts. The
// only third-party figures found for those conflicted with this page's own
// examPattern text, so they are deferred to the current bulletin instead.
EXAM_FAQS['nclex'] = [
  { question: "Which three organisations are involved in registering for the NCLEX?", answer: "Three separate organisations sit behind an NCLEX registration, and confusing them is the most common early mistake. NCSBN owns and writes the examination. Pearson VUE handles candidate registration and delivers the test at test centres. Your Nursing Regulatory Body - the state board of nursing where you intend to practise - decides whether you are eligible at all. NCSBN does not take registrations itself and will point you to the board for the state where you plan to work. Source: nclex.com, ncsbn.org" },
  { question: "What is the official order of steps to register for the NCLEX?", answer: "The published order runs: apply for licensure or registration with your Nursing Regulatory Body first, then meet all of that board's eligibility requirements, and only after that register for the examination with Pearson VUE. Registering with Pearson VUE needs your programme code, an email address and an acceptable form of payment. Registering before your board has cleared you leaves you mid-process with an authorisation to test you cannot yet use, so work the board's list first and treat Pearson VUE as the last step rather than the first. Source: NCLEX Candidate Bulletin (nclex.com), nclex.com" },
  { question: "Does NCSBN set the NCLEX eligibility rules, or does my state board?", answer: "Your state board sets them, not NCSBN. NCSBN directs candidates to the Nursing Regulatory Body for the jurisdiction where they plan to practise, and the detail varies by board, so the same nursing degree can carry different documentation steps in different states. Treat your own board's page as the authority on your case rather than an aggregator, a forum thread or a coaching site. Source: ncsbn.org" },
  { question: "Which official NCLEX documents should I read before I register?", answer: "Two: the NCSBN test plan for the current cycle, and the NCLEX Candidate Bulletin published through Pearson VUE. Both are reissued as the examination evolves, and the item types candidates practise on third-party platforms are frequently a revision behind what the current plan describes. NCSBN publishes test plans by year, so confirm the plan you have actually downloaded matches the sitting you are registering for rather than reusing last year's PDF. Source: ncsbn.org, nclex.com" },
  { question: "How is the NCLEX scored, and why do different sources quote different numbers of questions?", answer: "The NCLEX is computer-adaptive and scored pass or fail - there is no numeric score to aim at, so a practice predictor result is not a grade you can bank on. The exam ends once the algorithm decides you have demonstrated the required level of competence, which is why two candidates at the same table answer different numbers of items. The client-needs categories in the test plan describe what is being assessed, not a fixed number of questions per paper. If you need the current item counts and the decision rule that applies to your exam, read them from the current candidate bulletin rather than from a blog that has not been updated. Source: ncsbn.org, nclex.com" }
];

// HKDSE (Hong Kong Diploma of Secondary Education). Added 2220Z to replace the
// generic fallback, which named no administering body and no reporting system.
// Every claim below was read from hkeaa.edu.hk on 2026-10-19 (/en/hkdse/,
// /en/, /en/sa_tsa/). Intents chosen to NOT duplicate hkdse.ts, which already
// covers paper patterns, SBA weighting, eligibility and registration windows.
// Deliberately NOT stated: the 2027 fee amounts. The HKEAA publishes them in a
// PDF press release and PDF extraction is disabled on this host, so the figure
// is routed to the authority's own calculator rather than guessed at.
EXAM_FAQS['hkdse'] = [
  { question: "What is the Territory-wide System Assessment (TSA), and does it affect my HKDSE result?", answer: "The TSA is a separate exercise that the HKEAA has administered on behalf of the Education Bureau since 2004. It is aimed at schools rather than at individual candidates: the authority uses the results to show a school's teachers which parts of the programme need another look, so teaching plans can be adjusted for the following cohort. It sits alongside the public examination and the school-based assessment that make up your own subject results, and the same authority runs all three. How it relates to your individual reporting depends on the arrangements in force for your year group, so read the current Subject Assessment Framework for each of your subjects rather than assuming from an older guide. Source: hkeaa.edu.hk" },
  { question: "How are HKDSE results reported, and how does that become a JUPAS admission score?", answer: "The HKEAA reports subject results through Standards-referenced Reporting, and it publishes its own animated walkthrough of that grading process for candidates and parents. What travels onward to university admission is not the raw mark count but the grade awarded against that reporting scale. For a candidate who also wants a place through the university admissions system, the practical step is the published conversion table: each subject grade maps to a numeric score, and each participating institution then applies its own programme formula, usually over a best set of subjects rather than all of them. That is why two candidates with similar totals can receive different offers. Confirm the live conversion table with the admissions operator and the programme formula with the institution. Source: hkeaa.edu.hk" },
  { question: "What will the HKDSE examination fees cost me, and where is the official figure?", answer: "Fees are reset for each cycle, so treat any number written down earlier as out of date. The HKEAA announced the examination and supplementary fees for the 2027 sitting on 31 July, publishing both a press release and a short explainer, and it runs an online exam fee calculator built for the 2027 cycle so you can build up a total for the subjects and components you actually register for. Supplementing a paper carries its own charge, which is why no single flat figure is published. Run your own combination through the calculator rather than copying a total from a forum thread, and keep the press release, because that is the document that settles a query later. Source: hkeaa.edu.hk" },
  { question: "Which Other Languages subjects count towards the HKDSE, and can they help with civil service entry?", answer: "Five Other Languages subjects were brought under revised examination arrangements introduced in 2025, and the HKEAA has since widened both the list and the consequences. Results in this category have been accepted as entry requirements for relevant civil service grades, which turns the subject from an academic choice into a recruitment one for a slice of candidates. Urdu has been added to the group, and the authority has signalled that Hindi will be offered from 2028. Check the recognition pages for the current member list and for the civil service grades each result now satisfies, because both the roster and the grades that accept it have shifted across recent cycles. Source: hkeaa.edu.hk" },
  { question: "Which official HKEAA resources should I bookmark before I plan the HKDSE?", answer: "Four, all currently maintained. The Introduction to the HKDSE booklet is the standing reference for assessment and examination arrangements and for how the qualification is recognised abroad; the version published in May 2026 is the latest. The video library explains the grading process in short form, which beats hunting for the relevant paragraph inside a rule document. The Diagnostic Feedback System is the authority's own practice platform, drawing multiple-choice banks from ten HKDSE subjects and returning an estimated grade, a strengths-and-weaknesses report and the key knowledge points behind wrong answers. The newsletter also carries dated announcements, including the move to online applications for certification of exam results in February 2026. Source: hkeaa.edu.hk" }
];

// UCAT — questions the module does not answer: the once-per-cycle rule, who is
// barred from sitting, where it can be sat, how results reach universities, and
// the dated 2026 window. Every fact read from ucat.ac.uk on 19 October 2026.
EXAM_FAQS['ucat'] = [
  { question: "Can I sit the UCAT twice in the same cycle to get a better score?", answer: "No. The Consortium allows one sitting only — you may sit the test once in any test cycle — so a second attempt inside the same window is not available. That single sitting also removes the tactic of treating an early date as a free practice run, because the Consortium states the test is the same difficulty throughout the cycle rather than easing by date. Choosing when to sit is therefore a personal trade-off rather than a scoring strategy: the Consortium observes that some candidates take a July date while still in exam mode, while others prefer to prepare across the summer before a later sitting. Because the result cannot be improved inside the round, every hour of practice has to happen before the one date you hold. Source: ucat.ac.uk" },
  { question: "Can I sit the UCAT if I tutor, write content for, or hold a financial interest in a UCAT coaching company?", answer: "No, and this is a category the Consortium names explicitly rather than leaving open to interpretation. Individuals with an affiliation with a UCAT coaching business are not eligible to sit, and the examples given are acting as a tutor, working as a content developer, or holding some financial interest in a coaching business — so a stake-holding route that never involves teaching is covered too. Checks may be made to identify and cancel tests booked by ineligible candidates, which makes booking first and hoping to resolve it later a poor trade in a cycle you can only sit once. The Consortium does note that alternative entry requirements apply to some groups of applicants, which is a question for the individual university rather than the testing body. Source: ucat.ac.uk" },
  { question: "Where can I sit the UCAT, and what if the nearest test centre is a long way away?", answer: "The test is delivered in Pearson test centres across the UK and in more than 130 countries worldwide, and the Consortium links a Pearson Test Centre Locator so you can search from wherever you are based — that reach matters for candidates who would otherwise be priced out by travel. Where reaching a centre is genuinely difficult, the reasons the Consortium lists are distance, pandemic, war, civil unrest or natural disaster, and its instruction is to read the online proctored testing information for OnVUE UCAT before booking rather than assume a test centre is unavoidable. One boundary is worth knowing: this is the UK test, and applicants to Australian or New Zealand universities are referred to UCAT ANZ, which is run separately. Source: ucat.ac.uk" },
  { question: "Do I have to send my UCAT score to universities or attach it to my UCAS application?", answer: "Neither, and candidates who assume they must burn application time on work nobody needs from them. The Consortium delivers your result directly to the universities you choose, and universities accept only results provided directly by the UCAT Office — a self-uploaded score will not stand in for that. You are not required to pass the result on to your choices or to include it on your UCAS application, and after the 15 October application deadline the Consortium runs a data matching exercise with UCAS to identify which applications need a UCAT result, so you never have to supply your UCAS PID or your list of choices. You still receive your own copy: a score report is given to you before you leave the Pearson VUE test centre. Source: ucat.ac.uk" },
  { question: "When are the UCAT dates, and when did booking close for the 2026 cycle?", answer: "For the 2026 cycle, registration opened on 20 May 2026 at 14:00 UK time and booking on 23 June 2026 at 14:00, with testing running from 13 July to the last test day on 24 September 2026. Access arrangement applications closed on 10 September 2026, the bursary scheme on the same 15:00 deadline as the last test day, and booking and registration both closed on 16 September 2026 at 15:00 — a deadline the Consortium says it makes no exceptions to. The UCAS deadline is 15 October 2026 and results reach universities in early November. Instructions for the 2027 cycle had not been published when this was checked, so confirm the next window on the Consortium's test dates page rather than assuming these dates repeat. Source: ucat.ac.uk" }
];

// UCAT ANZ — questions the module does not answer: how the scale score is
// actually produced, why Situational Judgement sits outside the Total, the
// dated 2026 window, the fee ladder, and the one-year validity rule. Every fact
// read from ucat.edu.au on 19 October 2026.
EXAM_FAQS['ucat-anz'] = [
  { question: "How is my UCAT ANZ score actually produced, and is it a percentage?", answer:
    "Your UCAT ANZ score is neither a percentage nor a raw count of correct answers on its own. You are marked on the number of correct answers you give, with no negative marking, so an incorrect answer costs you nothing. Because the three cognitive subtests contain different numbers of questions, the Consortium converts raw marks into scale scores that share a common range from 300 to 900, which is what makes a Decision Making result comparable with a Quantitative Reasoning result. Verbal Reasoning has its own presentation detail worth knowing: your performance on one question does not influence which other questions are presented. All four subtests are scored this way. Source: ucat.edu.au" },
  { question: "Does my Situational Judgement score count towards my Total Scaled Score?", answer:
    "No, and this is the single most misunderstood part of UCAT ANZ scoring. The Total Scaled Score is the sum of three subtests only, Verbal Reasoning, Decision Making and Quantitative Reasoning, producing a range from 900 to 2700. Situational Judgement is excluded from that total and reported separately on the same 300 to 900 scale. It means a strong Situational Judgement result cannot lift your Total at all, and a university weighting the Total is not reading your SJ number. Marking also differs between subtests, because single-answer questions are worth one mark while the multiple-statement Decision Making questions are worth two, with one mark awarded for a partially correct response. Source: ucat.edu.au" },
  { question: "What were the UCAT ANZ 2026 booking deadlines, and when is the test sat?", answer:
    "For UCAT ANZ 2026, candidates applying for entry in 2027 should note that booking opened on 3 March 2026 and the testing window ran from 1 July to 5 August 2026 at Pearson VUE test centres. The Consortium states plainly that it makes no exceptions to its deadlines, and every deadline time is 11.59pm Australian Eastern Standard Time. The main cut-offs in that cycle were concession applications on 11 May, Access Arrangements applications on 15 May, the booking deadline on 15 May, a late booking period from 16 to 29 May, a final late booking period from 30 May to 5 June, and cancellation on 12 June 2026. Source: ucat.edu.au" },
  { question: "What does the UCAT ANZ test fee cost, and what do late or cancelled bookings cost?", answer:
    "The test fee is AUD$335 if you sit the UCAT ANZ in Australia or New Zealand, and AUD$405 if you sit it overseas. A concession fee of AUD$240 applies in Australia only, and it requires an approved Concession Scheme voucher code obtained before booking rather than claimed afterwards. Booking late is expensive in stages, because a late fee adds AUD$85 for bookings made between 16 and 29 May and a final late fee adds AUD$185 between 30 May and 5 June, and neither late fee is refundable. A refund costs AUD$50 deducted from the test fee, while rescheduling after 11.59pm AEST on 29 June costs AUD$25. All fees are charged in Australian dollars. Source: ucat.edu.au" },
  { question: "How do UCAT ANZ results reach universities, and how long is my score valid?", answer:
    "Results reach universities without any action from you, because the UCAT ANZ Office provides results for all candidates directly to the Consortium universities in early September. What you supply in a university application is your UCAT ANZ ID in the ANZ12345678 format, which appears on your Score Report and in your Pearson account, and you should check each university's own procedures and deadlines separately. Pearson emails you access to your own Score Report within 24 hours of sitting the test. Decisively, UCAT ANZ 2026 results are valid for the 2026 university admissions cycle only, meaning entry to university in 2027, and they do not carry over into a later year. Source: ucat.edu.au" },
];
// TMUA — Test of Mathematics for University Admission.
// Sourced from esat-tmua.ac.uk on 19 October 2026. Note that tmua.co.uk is an
// unrelated third-party prep platform, not the awarding body; every claim below
// comes from the UAT-UK site, which the module already names as officialSource.
EXAM_FAQS['tmua'] = [
  { question: "What level of mathematics does the TMUA actually test?", answer:
    "The TMUA is not pitched at A-level Mathematics, and that single fact resets most revision plans. UAT-UK states that the test is based on content typically covered within Higher Level GCSE mathematics courses or AS level mathematics, which means the paper is built on algebra, functions, coordinate geometry, trigonometry, calculus foundations, statistics and probability rather than on the long structured exercises of an A-level course. Candidates frequently buy pure, further or mechanics A-level resources because those are the books they already own, then spend weeks on content the paper never sets. A Higher Level GCSE textbook is a sufficient syllabus; past papers are the resource worth buying, because the paper tests short applied and reasoning items rather than extended proofs. Source: esat-tmua.ac.uk" },
  { question: "What free preparation materials does UAT-UK publish for the TMUA?", answer:
    "Three, all free on the UAT-UK site: the test specification, which is the document the paper is actually written from; Notes on Logic and Proof, which covers the elementary logic and proof material Paper 2 assesses; and a practice test. UAT-UK's own advice is to familiarise yourself with the format and style of the questions, then answer the practice test under timed conditions, and that last clause is the one candidates skip. Read the specification as a coverage checklist against what you can already do rather than as revision material. The practice test earns its value only on a clock, because twenty questions in seventy-five minutes is three minutes forty-five seconds an item and untimed practice hides precisely the arithmetic under pressure that costs marks in the hall. Source: esat-tmua.ac.uk" },
  { question: "How many universities use the TMUA, and what happens if registration is compulsory?", answer:
    "Seven. UAT-UK states that there are seven universities using the TMUA for selection to some courses in Mathematics, Economics or Computer Science, and the requirement is course-specific rather than university-wide, so it lives on the individual course page and not on the institution name. The consequence is given in the same terms as the registration rule: failure to register for the TMUA where it is compulsory may invalidate your application. The word to notice is may rather than will, and the safe reading for a candidate is that an untaken compulsory admissions test is treated as an incomplete application instead of a discretionary judgement. Read your course page before you plan your revision, not afterwards. Source: esat-tmua.ac.uk" },
  { question: "What does a TMUA bursary cover, and when must I apply for one?", answer:
    "Where one is granted, a bursary covers the full cost of the test, and eligibility is limited to UK candidates in financial need who can meet the criteria and provide the required documentation. Two timing rules govern it and UAT-UK states both in absolute terms. You must apply before you book your test, and the full-cost promise is a pre-booking benefit rather than a refund, because UAT-UK says it cannot reimburse you after you have paid for a test. Access arrangements follow the same deadline and carry the same message, with one addition: if you require them, UAT-UK cannot make adjustments to the test when you arrive at the test centre, so anything needed must be agreed in advance of the sitting and evidenced by documentation. Source: esat-tmua.ac.uk" },
  { question: "Which TMUA sitting can I take if I am applying to Cambridge or Oxford?", answer:
    "For 2027 entry there are two sittings, and the second carries a restriction. Test Sitting 1, 12 to 16 October 2026, applies to all candidates for 2027 entry. Test Sitting 2, 4 to 8 January 2027, is not applicable for Cambridge or Oxford applicants unless you are applying to a mature college with a January admissions deadline at Cambridge, or to an Oxford Foundation Year programme that also has a January deadline. Everyone else taking those two courses sits in October, so the January sitting is not a backup option for them. Booking for January opens at 3pm BST on 26 October 2026, and you create your UAT-UK account from 1 June, so register before the window opens. Source: esat-tmua.ac.uk" },
];
// USMLE — United States Medical Licensing Examination.
// Sourced from usmle.org and the 2027 Bulletin of Information PDF
// (usmle.org/sites/default/files/2026-08/2027Bulletin.pdf), both read 19 October 2026.
// The 2027 Bulletin governs applicants with a 2027 eligibility period; its Step 3
// two-day structure is stated as such below and is NOT asserted as today's format.
EXAM_FAQS['usmle'] = [
  { question: "Can I take USMLE Step 1 and Step 2 CK in any order, and what do I need before Step 3?", answer:
    "The first two are free to sequence. The Bulletin states that if you meet the eligibility requirements you may take Step 1 and Step 2 CK in any sequence, so sitting Step 2 CK first is legitimate when your clinical exposure is already strong. Step 3 is different: you may take it only after passing both. It also carries its own requirements, namely an MD or DO degree or its equivalent and, for graduates of medical schools outside the United States, a valid and unexpired ECFMG Certificate. The program additionally recommends completing, or being near completion of, at least one postgraduate training year in accredited US graduate medical education beforehand. That is advice rather than a cut-off, but ignoring it is how eligibility problems surface. Source: usmle.org" },
  { question: "Can I sit USMLE Step 3 in India, or do I have to travel to the United States?", answer:
    "You have to travel. The Bulletin states plainly that Step 1, Step 2 CK and Step 3 are administered at Prometric centres and that Step 3 is administered only in the United States and its territories. For an international medical graduate that single line carries more planning weight than any other rule in the programme, because it turns Step 3 from a one-day appointment into an international trip with visa, travel and accommodation costs to budget while you are still doing residency interviews. The same paragraph advises scheduling early at your preferred centre, since appointments at some test centres fill up quickly. For the 2027 eligibility period Step 3 runs over two days, so allow two. Source: usmle.org" },
  { question: "How many times can I attempt a USMLE Step before I am locked out of the whole sequence?", answer:
    "Fewer than most candidates assume, and the limit is final rather than a cooling-off. If you have attempted a Step four or more times, including incomplete attempts, and have not passed, you are ineligible to apply for any Step in the sequence, not merely the one you failed. Attempts at the formerly administered Step 2 Clinical Skills count toward that limit even though the exam no longer exists. Within it you may not take the same Step more than three times in a 12-month period. Passing does not end the risk: fail a retake of a Step you already passed and you are no longer eligible to take Step 3. Source: usmle.org" },
  { question: "What can cause a USMLE result to be reported as incomplete instead of scored?", answer:
    "Not opening every block. The Bulletin states that if you do not open every block of your examination, it may not be scored and the attempt may be reported as incomplete on your USMLE transcript, which is why an unanswered trailing block is not a neutral act. An incomplete attempt still counts towards the four-attempt ceiling. The same paragraph adds a Step 3-specific trap: for the 2027 two-day format, taking Day 1 and not Day 2 may have the exam reported as incomplete, so attending only the first day of Step 3 is a live risk rather than a partial credit. Unusual response patterns are analysed separately, and candidates whose score pattern prompts review may be asked to explain their testing behaviour. Source: usmle.org" },
  { question: "How do I get my USMLE score to a residency programme or an employer?", answer:
    "Not by forwarding the score report. Score reports are only for your own use, and to have your exam history reported to any third party you must request an official USMLE transcript sent directly to them. Transcripts come from FSMB and carry an associated fee; your transcript includes your biographical information, complete examination history and, where applicable, findings of irregular behaviour. Candidates using ERAS should follow the instructions of the application owner for sending transcripts to US training programmes. Separately, NBME reports results to the medical school that registered you, so if you do not want Step 1 or Step 2 CK results reaching your school you must email the request at least 10 business days before your test date, once per administration. Source: usmle.org" },
];

// LNAT — National Admissions Test for Law (UK undergraduate law).
// Sourced from lnat.ac.uk on 19 October 2026: /what-is-lnat/,
// /what-is-lnat/test-format/, /what-is-lnat/results/,
// /what-is-lnat/do-i-need-to-sit-the-test/,
// /registration/dates-and-deadlines/ and /registration/paying-for-your-test/.
// Content is managed by the LNAT Consortium; the test is administered by
// Pearson VUE under contract to LNAT. No university count is asserted: the
// participant list on lnat.ac.uk is rendered dynamically and did not resolve
// in a plain fetch on the sourcing date.
EXAM_FAQS['lnat'] = [
  { question: "Does the LNAT essay add to my LNAT score, or is it read separately?", answer:
    "Only the multiple-choice section produces a score. The Consortium states that Section B is not marked by the test centre and does not contribute to your LNAT score, so the mark out of 42 that universities compare comes entirely from Section A. That makes the essay a separate channel rather than a rounding error, and its value is real. There is no fixed weight attached to it: some universities use the essay as the basis for interview questions, others compare it with your personal statement and school or college report on the UCAS form, and others use it to separate borderline candidates. Write to the question you are actually given rather than to an imagined rubric. Source: lnat.ac.uk" },
  { question: "When do LNAT results get emailed, and do universities see them before I do?", answer:
    "Twice a year, and the sitting date decides which one. Candidates who take the test on or before 26 January receive their results in mid February; candidates who take it after 26 January receive results in mid August. The Consortium states that no specific dates can be given, so plan in weeks rather than to a calendar day. The more useful fact for planning is the sequence: your score and essay are made available to participating universities before you receive them, so decisions are already moving while you are still waiting for the email. That is also why a late application needs checking first, because universities may not accept an LNAT result taken as late as July. Source: lnat.ac.uk" },
  { question: "What are the LNAT deadlines for applicants to Cambridge or Oxford?", answer:
    "They fall a full quarter earlier than everyone else's, and the window is not recoverable inside the same cycle. The Consortium states that Cambridge and Oxford applicants must submit the UCAS form and sit the LNAT on or before 15 October 2026, which also fixes the booking window at 1 August to 15 September 2026. One exception sits in the same table: the Foundation Year in Law at the University of Oxford requires the test to be sat on or before 13 January 2027. Testing begins on 1 September 2026 and UCAS applications open in mid September. Read the cycle year as carefully as the date, because different test dates draw different papers. Source: lnat.ac.uk" },
  { question: "What are the January LNAT deadlines if I am not applying to Cambridge or Oxford?", answer:
    "Book your test slot before 20 January 2027, submit your UCAS form by 6:00pm UK time on 13 January 2027, and sit the test on or before 25 January 2027. Two universities break from that pattern: applicants to the University of Bristol and the University of Durham must register and book by 13 January 2027, submit the UCAS form by the same day, and sit on or before 13 January 2027. The London group is earlier again, with applicants to King's College London, the London School of Economics and University College London required to sit on or before 31 December 2026. Under UCAS rules universities are permitted but not required to consider late applications. Source: lnat.ac.uk" },
  { question: "What does the LNAT cost, and how do I pay if my card fails verification?", answer:
    "The fee follows the test centre, not your nationality or your place of residence: 75 GBP at UK and EU test centres and 120 GBP at centres outside the EU, always charged in pounds sterling. Payment is taken at the time of booking with no exceptions, by major credit card or by Visa and MasterCard debit cards that do not require PIN validation. Candidates paying from a named list of countries, including Pakistan, Nigeria, Kenya, Bangladesh and Ghana, must use an LNAT voucher and cannot pay by card at all, regardless of which bank issued the card. A voucher is exchanged for a cheque, bank transfer or cash and takes at least a week to process. Source: lnat.ac.uk" }
];

// KASNEB CPA (kenya) — official source https://www.kasneb.or.ke/cpa and https://www.kasneb.or.ke/faqs,
// read 2026-10-19. Paper codes and entry requirement verified verbatim against the KASNEB CPA page.
EXAM_FAQS['kasneb-cpa'] = [
  { question: "Can you defer a KASNEB exam, and what does it cost?", answer:
    "Yes, but only on three grounds KASNEB recognises: illness during the examination dates, the death of an immediate close family member before you sit, and a timetable clash with another institution. A clash between two KASNEB sittings is not deferrable, because the April, August and December windows are the only ones offered. The deferment fee is 15 per cent of your total booking fees, so it is charged on everything you booked for that sitting rather than as a flat amount, which means deferring one paper out of six still costs a percentage of all six. Applications go through the student portal rather than by email or in person, and the fee is the part candidates miss: an illness documented late still attracts it. Source: kasneb.or.ke" },
  { question: "What documents do I need to bring to a KASNEB examination?", answer:
    "Three, and the first is the one candidates forget. You must carry your authority to sit the examination letter, which KASNEB calls the timetable, and it is generated from the portal after booking rather than posted to you in advance. Alongside it you bring a National Identity card or a passport, and a birth certificate only in special cases, so the routine pair is the letter and the identity document. The timetable also serves as the authority for which papers and which centre you are sitting at, so leaving it behind means turning up to a hall where your registration cannot be verified. Source: kasneb.or.ke" },
  { question: "How soon can I move my KASNEB examination centre?", answer:
    "At least two months before the examination dates, which is the rule rather than a preference, and KASNEB does consider late requests case by case rather than refusing them outright. The two-month window matters because booking, sitting and reallocation are separate events, and a late request leaves the invigilator allocation already built around the original centre. The submission route is the student portal, the same route used for booking, registration, exemption and renewal, so there is no separate centre-change form. Book the centre you can actually reach rather than planning to relocate the exam later. Source: kasneb.or.ke" },
  { question: "When exactly is the KASNEB examination booking deadline?", answer:
    "KASNEB sets a deadline per sitting and states the outer limit: it may not be later than thirty days before the date of the next examinations. That is a ceiling, not the standard timetable, so the published notice for a given sitting can and does close earlier, and the December 2026 sitting carried an examination-entry close of 30 October against a window beginning on 30 November. Because the limit is expressed relative to the next sitting rather than as a fixed annual date, a candidate waiting for the pattern from a previous year can be a month late. The current deadline is specified in the notice for that sitting. Source: kasneb.or.ke" },
  { question: "Do KASNEB credits expire if I take longer than planned?", answer:
    "For papers passed under the new syllabus, credits are retained without limit. KASNEB states this as a retention rule rather than as a validity period, so a paper passed years ago under the current syllabus keeps its credit indefinitely and you are not forced to retake it because the qualification took longer than expected. Read the rule as scoped to the new syllabus it names: a credit sitting under an older paper code from before the CA11 to CA37WP structure is not obviously covered by a statement about new-syllabus papers, so anyone resuming on grandfathered codes should ask KASNEB rather than assume the unlimited retention applies to them. Source: kasneb.or.ke" }
];

// HSC Mathematics Advanced (australia) — official source
// https://curriculum.nsw.edu.au/learning-areas/mathematics/mathematics-advanced-11-12-2024/overview
// and the NESA sample examination materials PDF for the 2024 syllabus, read 2026-10-20.
// Exam structure (100 marks, Section I 10 marks, Section II 90 marks, 10 min reading +
// 3 hours working, first HSC exam 2027, no common items) read verbatim from that PDF.
EXAM_FAQS['hsc-mathematics-advanced'] = [
  { question: "When does the 2024 HSC Mathematics Advanced syllabus actually get examined?", answer:
    "The first HSC examination under the 2024 syllabus is held in 2027, and that is the date to plan backwards from. The two dates that get confused are the two NESA publishes side by side: teaching of the new syllabus for Year 12 begins in Term 4 of 2026, and the first HSC examination for it is in 2027. So the gap is a full academic year, not a term, and a Year 12 cohort starting the new syllabus in 2026 sits their external HSC in 2027. The syllabus replaces the Mathematics Advanced Stage 6 Syllabus (2017), so a 2027 candidate is the first for whom past papers from earlier years are indicative of content rather than of format, because the exam shape itself changes with the syllabus. Source: curriculum.nsw.edu.au" },
  { question: "What is the HSC Mathematics Advanced exam format under the 2024 syllabus?", answer:
    "One written paper worth 100 marks, with 10 minutes reading time and 3 hours working time. It splits into two sections. Section I is 10 marks across Questions 1 to 10, about 15 minutes, answered on a separate multiple-choice answer sheet. Section II is 90 marks across Questions 11 to 35, about 2 hours 45 minutes, answered in the spaces provided on the paper. Two details printed in the general instructions matter before exam day: calculators approved by NESA may be used, and a reference sheet is provided at the back of the paper. Section II asks you to show relevant mathematical reasoning and calculations, which is where method marks are won. Source: curriculum.nsw.edu.au" },
  { question: "Do HSC maths exams share common questions any more?", answer:
    "Not under the 2024 syllabus for Mathematics Advanced, and that is a real break from what earlier candidates learned. NESA states that the Mathematics Advanced examination does not include items common with other Mathematics HSC examinations. The dropped arrangement is specific: under the 2017 syllabus the exam shared 20 to 25 marks of common items with Mathematics Standard 2, spread across both sections. This changes revision advice in one way. Sitting both Standard 2 and Advanced used to mean partly the same paper, so practice carried across; under the 2024 syllabus it does not, and a Standard 2 candidate revising from Advanced papers is revising for a different examination. NESA still uses past HSC questions to illustrate question types, so past papers remain worth working through for style. Source: curriculum.nsw.edu.au" },
  { question: "Is Working Mathematically a separate component I should revise on its own?", answer:
    "No, and treating it as one costs you time. Under the 2024 syllabus Working Mathematically is embedded in the outcomes and content of the syllabus rather than sitting as its own assessed strand. The content itself is organised into 7 areas of study: Functions, Trigonometric functions, Exponential and logarithmic functions, Sequences and series, Calculus, Statistical analysis, and Financial mathematics. NESA describes the organisation as highlighting the important role Working Mathematically plays across all areas, and that the strengthened connections between concepts are reflected in how the outcomes and content are arranged. The practical consequence for revision is that reasoning, communication, problem solving and mathematical modelling are marked inside the questions in those seven areas, not scored on a separate rubric. Source: curriculum.nsw.edu.au" },
  { question: "How is an HSC Mathematics Advanced result reported?", answer:
    "As a performance band from 1 to 6, and NESA publishes the descriptions so you can read what each band means before you sit rather than guessing at the boundaries. Band 6 describes a student who typically demonstrates extensive knowledge, understanding and skills appropriate to the course, integrates and applies concepts, skills and techniques consistently and accurately across familiar and unfamiliar situations, and constructs and analyses mathematical models. At the other end, a performance reported as Band 1 indicates that the minimum standard expected was not demonstrated. Bands 2 to 5 carry their own published descriptions. Year 11 reporting is separate and uses a Common Grade Scale across 5 grade levels, so a Year 11 grade is not a band and should not be read as one. Source: curriculum.nsw.edu.au" }
];

EXAM_FAQS['hsc-english-advanced'] = [
  { question: "When does the 2024 HSC English Advanced syllabus actually get examined?", answer:
    "NESA states that the first HSC examination under the English Advanced 11-12 Syllabus (2024) will be held in 2027, and that is the milestone to work back from when planning. The year that causes the confusion is the teaching year, because Year 12 classes on the new syllabus begin in Term 4 of 2026. A cohort that starts it in that year therefore sits its external examination a whole academic year afterwards rather than in the same calendar year. NESA also notes that both English Advanced papers retain the structure of the 2019 papers, so past HSC questions stay worth working through for question types even though the syllabus content itself is new. Source: curriculum.nsw.edu.au" },
  { question: "What exactly is the HSC English Advanced exam format under the 2024 syllabus?", answer:
    "Two papers totalling 100 marks. Paper 1, Texts and human experiences, is worth 40 marks with 10 minutes of reading time and 1 hour 30 minutes of working time: Section I is 20 marks across Questions 1 to 5, about 45 minutes, written directly on the answer spaces provided, and Section II is 20 marks for Question 6, about 45 minutes, written in the Section II Writing Booklet. Paper 2 is worth 60 marks with 5 minutes of reading time and 2 hours of working time, split into three 20-mark sections of about 40 minutes each, being Section I Textual conversations, Section II Critical study of literature, and Section III The craft of writing. Everything written for Paper 2 goes into the Paper 2 Writing Booklet. Source: curriculum.nsw.edu.au" },
  { question: "How many prescribed texts do I study for HSC English Advanced, and what are the rules?", answer:
    "Four prescribed texts for the Year 12 course, and the selection rule is where students get caught out. You must draw at least one text from each of three categories: prose fiction; poetry; and one of drama, nonfiction, film or media. At least one of the texts selected must be authored by Shakespeare, so a student who wants prose fiction and poetry still has to find a third slot that satisfies the play requirement. Year 11 has no prescribed texts at all, which means the text list only becomes a live constraint in Year 12. NESA states the HSC English prescriptions can be seen in the relevant focus areas of the syllabus. Source: curriculum.nsw.edu.au" },
  { question: "Do HSC English Advanced and English Standard share examination questions?", answer:
    "Yes, and the arrangement is specific rather than general. NESA states that at least three items in English Advanced Paper 1 Section I will be common to English Standard Paper 1 Section I. In the sample examination published for the 2024 syllabus, Questions 1, 2 and 3 of English Advanced are the items in common with Questions 3, 4 and 5 of the Standard paper. The practical consequence is that a student sitting both courses can carry some Paper 1 Section I preparation across, but the common items are confined to Section I of Paper 1 rather than spread across the papers, so the two courses are not interchangeable. Source: curriculum.nsw.edu.au" },
  { question: "How is the HSC English Advanced course structured in hours, and what must be completed first?", answer:
    "Year 11 and Year 12 are each 120 indicative hours, but split differently. Year 11 runs three units of 40 hours, opening with Reading to write: Transition to English Advanced and continuing through Narratives that shape our world and Critical study of literature. Year 12 runs four units of 30 hours, opening with Texts and human experiences and then covering textual conversation, literary criticism and composition. Completing the Year 11 course is a stated prerequisite for the Year 12 course rather than a recommendation. Across both years NESA requires the text selection to include Australian texts and texts authored by Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Peoples. Source: curriculum.nsw.edu.au" }
];

EXAM_FAQS['osslt'] = [
  { question: "What score do I need to pass the OSSLT?", answer:
    "EQAO scores the OSSLT with Item Response Theory, which places each student on a continuum of literacy ability and reports a scale score between 200 and 400. The minimum scale score for a successful outcome is 300, so 300 is the number to work backwards from when you are judging whether a practice attempt was good enough. The scale score is not a mark out of 400 and does not map directly onto the number of questions you answered correctly, because the procedure accounts for the difficulty of each question as well as the response itself. The outcome is reported as either successful or not yet successful rather than as a grade. Source: eqao.com" },
  { question: "How many questions are on the OSSLT, and how are they split between reading and writing?", answer:
    "The scored part of the test is 37 operational questions: 35 selected-response questions and two open-response questions. The reading component accounts for 23 of the selected-response questions plus one open-response reading question, and the writing component accounts for 12 selected-response writing questions plus one open-response opinion essay. The question types you meet are single-selection, multiple-selection, drag and drop, drop-down menu and checklist. Each test also carries a field-test component whose items are embedded in the session but are not used in determining results, which is why you may see items that look like extra questions. Source: eqao.com" },
  { question: "What exactly is the Opinion Essay, the OSSLT's long-writing task?", answer:
    "The Opinion Essay is the OSSLT's single long-writing open-response task, and EQAO describes it as one question composed of a series of paragraphs supporting an opinion. The student must write three or more paragraphs: an introduction, a development section that may run to more than one paragraph, and a conclusion. The topic is current and familiar to teenagers, and the published sample instructions ask for approximately 500 words. The response is typed into a text box and scored against two separate generic rubrics, Topic Development and Conventions, each with its own score codes. Older preparation material calls this task the Series of Paragraphs, which is why that name still circulates. Source: eqao.com" },
  { question: "When is the OSSLT written in the 2026-27 school year, and when do results arrive?", answer:
    "There are two administration windows each year. The Fall 2026 window runs from Tuesday 3 November 2026 to Monday 30 November 2026, and individual student results are reported by the end of January 2027. The Spring 2027 window runs from Tuesday 30 March 2027 to Monday 19 April 2027, with results reported by the end of May 2027. EQAO advises that the last two days of each window are kept for students who missed their originally scheduled dates or for extenuating circumstances, so a student who is unsuccessful on a first sitting still has a later date inside the same window. The test is written during the regular school day, and the school chooses the session times. Source: eqao.com" },
  { question: "If I do not pass the OSSLT, how can I still get my OSSD?", answer:
    "The Ontario Secondary School Literacy Course, the OSSLC, is the alternative route to the literacy graduation requirement, and the eligibility rule is specific. A student who has had two opportunities to write the OSSLT and has been unsuccessful at least once may enrol in the OSSLC. Principals also have discretion to allow a student to enrol before a second opportunity where they judge it to be in the student's best interest, and mature students may enrol directly. Completing either the OSSLT or the OSSLC satisfies the literacy requirement set by the Ministry of Education for the Ontario Secondary School Diploma, so an unsuccessful sitting does not end the diploma. Source: eqao.com" }
];

EXAM_FAQS['ruet'] = [
  { question: "What are the stages of the RUET admission test?", answer:
    "RUET admission is a two-stage process, and most preparation advice is written for only the first stage. The Preliminary Test is a multiple-choice paper, and RUET publishes its seat plan under a name that identifies the paper as MCQ. Candidates who clear the preliminary stage are then named on an eligible-candidates list, published separately for each applicant group. The second stage is a written test, for which RUET issues its own admit card, and the written test result is published before the final admission test result. Selection is completed online afterwards: for the 2025-26 session RUET set the subject-choice submission window from 10.00 AM on 3 February 2026 to 5.00 PM on 10 February 2026. Source: admission.ruet.ac.bd" },
  { question: "Is the RUET admission test sat in one sitting, or in shifts?", answer:
    "In shifts, and this is the detail that changes how you prepare. RUET publishes the preliminary test result as a separate result sheet for each of three shifts, and the eligible-candidate lists are released shift by shift as well, with the second shift labelled 12.00 PM to 01.00 PM and the third labelled 03.00 PM to 04.00 PM. The consequence for a candidate is that your seat in the hall, your result sheet and your shortlisting all follow the shift you were allotted, so you should confirm the shift on your own admit card rather than assuming that everyone in the hall starts at the same time. Source: admission.ruet.ac.bd" },
  { question: "What are the RUET applicant groups, and does the group affect the result?", answer:
    "Yes, it does. RUET admits candidates under named groups and publishes separate paperwork for each of them. The eligible-candidates lists are issued individually for Group-KA and Group-KHA, and the published result files are likewise separated, including a distinct file for tribal candidates. Because the shortlist, the results and the department allocation are all worked out group by group, the group recorded on your application is not a formality. It is also the one field RUET will not let you change freely: once you have paid the examination fee the group is locked, and the university's guidance is to submit a fresh application under the group you actually want if you have not paid yet. Source: admission.ruet.ac.bd" },
  { question: "How do I apply to RUET, and what should I prepare before I start?", answer:
    "The application is completed entirely online. RUET states that you cannot print the form and complete it by hand, and that a printable PDF of the application is generated for you from your dashboard once you have submitted. Have your photograph and signature ready to a specification, because the portal rejects files that miss it: the photo must be 300 by 350 pixels and no larger than 75 KB, and the signature 300 by 80 pixels and no larger than 20 KB. Payment runs through the SSL Commerz gateway and accepts cards, internet banking, and mobile banking services including bKash, Rocket and Nagad. On submission you receive an email and an SMS carrying your application ID and password. Source: admission.ruet.ac.bd" },
  { question: "What can I still change after I pay the RUET application fee?", answer:
    "Two things lock the moment the examination fee is paid. The first is your examination centre, which RUET states cannot be changed once the fee is paid, so it is worth choosing carefully on the form rather than treating it as provisional. The second is your group. Personal details are handled differently, and the route for fixing them is not re-editing the form. If your name, your parents' name or your date of birth appears wrong, you complete and pay the application as it stands and then email the university, putting your application ID in the subject line, describing the problem, and attaching the form, the money receipt and supporting proof. Source: admission.ruet.ac.bd" }
];

EXAM_FAQS['gamsat'] = [
  { question: "What are the three GAMSAT sections, and how much time is allowed for each?", answer:
    "The two multiple-choice sections have published time limits that decide how you pace the paper. Reasoning in Humanities and Social Sciences is 62 questions in 100 minutes, about 1.6 minutes per item. Reasoning in Biological and Physical Sciences is 75 questions in 150 minutes, exactly 2 minutes per item. Written Communication is run as a 65-minute remote-proctored component containing two separate 30-minute writing tasks, and it is sat before the multiple-choice sections rather than with them. That gives 250 minutes of multiple-choice testing in total, so section timing is not academic bookkeeping: the science section is the slower-paced of the two by a clear margin, and candidates who carry the humanities pace into it run out of time on the data-heavy items. Source: gamsat.acer.org (Information Booklet 2026)" },
  { question: "Is the GAMSAT Written Communication section sat at a test centre?", answer:
    "No, and this is the detail most preparation material gets wrong. ACER delivers the Written Communication section by remote proctoring for all test takers, so the writing is done at home on your own machine, not in a test centre. Remote proctoring has two categories: Written Communication, which applies to every test taker, and test takers located more than three hours from a test centre or in a country where no test centre is offered. The two multiple-choice sections are the opposite — they are completed at in-person test centres approximately three weeks after the Written Communication window, so a candidate's year involves a proctored writing sitting first and a test-centre visit later. Source: gamsat.acer.org (Information Booklet 2026)" },
  { question: "Which GAMSAT sitting should I plan around, and when will my score be released?", answer:
    "Both 2026 cycles have now been sat, which matters if you are reading this to plan ahead. The March 2026 cycle ran Written Communication 28 February - 1 March, then the two multiple-choice sections 20-22 March, with results released in late May. The September 2026 cycle ran Written Communication 22-23 August and the multiple-choice sections 11-13 September, with results due in mid November 2026. The next entry point is therefore the following March cycle. ACER publishes its dates and the day registration opens on gamsat.acer.org, and registration closes well before each test date with no applications accepted afterwards, so check that page rather than assuming a cycle is open. Source: gamsat.acer.org (Information Booklet 2026)" },
  { question: "How long is a GAMSAT result valid, and which sittings can I use for my application?", answer:
    "Validity is measured in consecutive years and is shorter outside Australia. GAMSAT results obtained from different sittings are directly comparable, because ACER transforms raw scores onto a scaled measurement scale, and a result can be used for applications to Australian universities for up to four consecutive years, against two consecutive years in the UK and Ireland. For an Australian application lodged in 2026 for a programme commencing in 2027, ACER accepts results from September 2022, March 2023, September 2023, March 2024, September 2024, March 2025, September 2025 and March 2026. If you are applying in the UK or Ireland, check your institution's own cycle list, since the two-year window changes which of those sittings still counts. Source: gamsat.acer.org (Information Booklet 2026)" },
  { question: "What does GAMSAT cost to sit, and can I change my booking after registering?", answer:
    "The registration fee is AUD$568 including GST in Australia, €378 in Ireland and £296 in the UK, paid in full by credit card at the time of registration, with MasterCard and Visa accepted. Sitting in Singapore adds a further AUD$208 overseas test levy. You can move your test centre or test day through your ACER account free of charge until standard registrations close; after that, up to the close of late registrations, a change costs AUD$114 in Australia, €83 in Ireland or £71 in the UK and is subject to availability. Past that point test dates and centres cannot be changed at all. Where a location has more than one venue you are allocated one automatically. Source: gamsat.acer.org (Information Booklet 2026)" }
];

EXAM_FAQS['wace-mathematics-methods'] = [
  { question: "How is WACE Mathematics Methods split across Year 11 and Year 12?", answer:
    "The course is built as four units, and where the split falls decides what is externally marked. Unit 1 and Unit 2 are taken in Year 11, where assessment is school-based and reported through your school rather than by SCSA. Unit 3 and Unit 4 are taken in Year 12, and the ATAR course examination is based on those two units only. A Year 12 revision plan that loops back through Unit 1 and Unit 2 material is therefore built on the wrong foundation: those units are never examined externally, while anything in Units 3 and 4 can appear in the paper. Map revision to the Year 12 units first, not the other way round. Source: senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au (Mathematics Methods ATAR Year 12 syllabus)" },
  { question: "Is the WACE Mathematics Methods exam one paper or two?", answer:
    "It is sat in two sections, and the calculator rule changes between them. The syllabus sets out a calculator-free section followed by a calculator-assumed section, and reading time is allowed before the paper begins. SCSA publishes a front cover for each section as part of the examination materials, which is a useful reminder that the two sittings are genuinely separate rather than one continuous paper. Practically, the calculator-free section is where the syllabus is tested on exact set-up, algebra and interpretation, because no machine can rescue a mis-stated step. Candidates who rehearse only with a calculator running usually lose those marks first. Source: senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au (Mathematics Methods ATAR Year 12 syllabus)" },
  { question: "Is WACE Mathematics Specialist the same exam as Mathematics Methods?", answer:
    "No. They are separate ATAR courses with separate examinations, and treating them as one paper is a common planning error. SCSA conducts distinct ATAR mathematics course examinations for Mathematics Applications, Mathematics Methods and Mathematics Specialist, so a Specialist student sits a different paper from a Methods student, drawn from different content. Both courses keep the same four-unit structure across Year 11 and Year 12, but Specialist pushes into the higher strands instead of repeating what Methods already covers. Their syllabi are not always the same vintage either: Mathematics Methods is published for teaching from January 2026, while Specialist carries an earlier refresh, which matters if you are mapping topics across the two. Source: senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au (Mathematics Specialist ATAR Year 12 syllabus)" },
  { question: "Where do I get official WACE maths past papers and the formula sheet?", answer:
    "Both come from the School Curriculum and Standards Authority rather than from your school. SCSA publishes past ATAR course examination papers for Mathematics Methods on its own site, together with a per-year formula sheet for the subject, and it releases marking advice and summary reports after each sitting. The formula sheet is the more useful of the two for planning, because it shows which results are supplied and which you are expected to derive, and that division is not identical from year to year. Work through a past paper and the formula sheet published for that same year together rather than mixing vintages. Source: senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au (past ATAR course exams, Mathematics Methods)" },
  { question: "Where is the WACE maths exam structure actually published?", answer:
    "In the course syllabus itself, rather than in a separate exam guide you have to hunt down. SCSA's WACE Manual explains that the relevant Curriculum Advisory Committee advises on the examination design brief for each ATAR course, and the department's circulars direct teachers to the examination design brief located in the course syllabus on the syllabus page. So the paper structure, the weighting and any technology rules are one click from the syllabus document itself. For dates, SCSA also issues the ATAR course written examinations timetable as a PDF each year, and personalised timetables are released through the SCSA student portal ahead of the examination period. Source: scsa.wa.edu.au (WACE Manual)" }
];


EXAM_FAQS['muet'] = [
  { question: "What does the MUET score scale actually run from, and is there a Band 6?", answer:
    "MUET reports an aggregated score out of 360 and converts it to a band topping out at 5+, not 6. Each of the four papers is marked out of 90, and the published mapping runs 331-360 for Band 5+, 294-330 for Band 5.0, 258-293 for Band 4.5, 211-257 for Band 4.0, 164-210 for Band 3.5 and 123-163 for Band 3.0, with lower bands beneath. Two consequences follow. There is no Band 6 to aim for, so a syllabus written against a 1-6 scale is describing a different exam. And because the top is stepped in halves, a programme asking for Band 4 means 211-257 aggregated rather than a fraction of the range. Source: portal.mpm.edu.my (MUET 800 Regulations and Test Specifications) " },
  { question: "How is MUET split into four papers, and what does each one test?", answer:
    "MUET is a written, invigilated test built as four papers, each weighted 25% and marked out of 90. Paper 800/1 Listening runs 50 minutes with 5 parts drawn from 7 texts and 30 questions, every text played twice. Paper 800/2 Speaking runs 30 minutes in two parts: an individual presentation after 2 minutes of preparation, then a group discussion with up to 4 candidates after 3 minutes of preparation, lasting 8 to 12 minutes. Paper 800/3 Reading runs 75 minutes with 7 parts, 10 texts and 40 questions. Paper 800/4 Writing runs 75 minutes with 2 tasks on separate pages. Listening, Reading and Writing fall on one day, while Speaking is sat on another, subject to room availability. Source: portal.mpm.edu.my (MUET 800 Regulations and Test Specifications) " },
  { question: "If I only sit two of the four MUET papers, will I still get a band score?", answer:
    "The regulations require all four components to be attempted before an overall band score is issued, and no certificate is awarded to a candidate who skips any one. This is the rule most easily overlooked, because a candidate who misses a paper through illness may assume a partial result is banked toward a later attempt. It is not. The aggregate is formed from all four papers, so an unattempted component removes the band rather than postponing it. Because Listening, Reading and Writing fall on one day and Speaking on another, treat the Speaking date as binding like the main sitting. Source: portal.mpm.edu.my (MUET 800 Regulations and Test Specifications) " },
  { question: "What does MUET cost to sit, and how long does a MUET result stay valid?", answer:
    "The MUET test fee is RM150 for Malaysian candidates and RM350 for non-Malaysian candidates. The separate online product, MUET on Demand, is priced higher at RM350 and RM550. Fees can be paid by credit card, internet banking or a purchased PIN, and the council reserves the right to withhold results from candidates who have not paid in full, so an unpaid fee turns a completed sitting into no certificate. Changing test centre after registering carries an administrative charge of RM40. Results released since 2009 remain valid for five years from the date of release, and a result may only be queried within one week of that release. Source: portal.mpm.edu.my (MUET 800 Regulations and Test Specifications) " },
  { question: "Which MUET paper is hardest to prepare for, and how should revision time be split?", answer:
    "Because all four papers carry identical 25% weightings and equal 90-mark maxima, revision time cannot be traded between them: each contributes the same 90 marks to a 360 aggregate, so a strong Reading score cannot rescue a failed Writing paper. The tightest constraint is Speaking, where Part 1 is a 2-minute presentation after only 2 minutes of preparation and Part 2 must run 8 to 12 minutes in a group of up to 4. That paper cannot be self-paced, so it is worth rehearsing to the clock first. Listening comes next, because its 50 minutes carry 30 questions across 7 texts with every text played twice, a condition practice material rarely reproduces. Source: portal.mpm.edu.my (MUET 800 Regulations and Test Specifications) " }
];
